      "text": "To solve the equation \\(7^{x+7} = 8^x\\), we start by expressing both sides of the equation with the same base. However, since 7 and 8 are not powers of each other, we will take the logarithm of both sides to eliminate the exponents. Let's use the natural logarithm (ln) for convenience:\n\n\\[\n\\ln(7^{x+7}) = \\ln(8^x)\n\\]\n\nBy the properties of logarithms, we can bring the exponents down:\n\n\\[\n(x+7) \\ln 7 = x \\ln 8\n\\]\n\nNext, we distribute the logarithms:\n\n\\[\nx \\ln 7 + 7 \\ln 7 = x \\ln 8\n\\]\n\nTo isolate \\(x\\), we first get all the \\(x\\)-terms on one side of the equation:\n\n\\[\nx \\ln 7 - x \\ln 8 = -7 \\ln 7\n\\]\n\nFactor out \\(x\\) on the left side:\n\n\\[\nx (\\ln 7 - \\ln 8) = -7 \\ln 7\n\\]\n\nSolve for \\(x\\) by dividing both sides by \\(\\ln 7 - \\ln 8\\):\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-7 \\ln 7}{\\ln 7 - \\ln 8}\n\\]\n\nWe are given that the solution is in the form \\(x = \\log_b 7^7\\). Therefore, we need to express \\(x\\) in terms of a logarithm with base \\(b\\):\n\n\\[\nx = \\log_b 7^7\n\\]\n\nBy the definition of logarithms, \\(\\log_b 7^7 = \\frac{\\ln 7^7}{\\ln b}\\). We know from the previous calculation that \\(x = \\frac{-7 \\ln 7}{\\ln 7 - \\ln 8}\\). Therefore, we can equate the two expressions for \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{-7 \\ln 7}{\\ln 7 - \\ln 8} = \\frac{\\ln 7^7}{\\ln b}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\ln 7^7 = 7 \\ln 7\\), we substitute this in:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{-7 \\ln 7}{\\ln 7 - \\ln 8} = \\frac{7 \\ln 7}{\\ln b}\n\\]\n\nWe can cancel \\(\\ln 7\\) from both sides (assuming \\(\\ln 7 \\neq 0\\)):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{-7}{\\ln 7 - \\ln 8} = \\frac{7}{\\ln b}\n\\]\n\nSimplifying, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{-1}{\\ln 7 - \\ln 8} = \\frac{1}{\\ln b}\n\\]\n\nThis implies:\n\n\\[\n\\ln b = -\\ln 7 + \\ln 8\n\\]\n\nUsing the properties of logarithms, we can combine the logarithms on the right side:\n\n\\[\n\\ln b = \\ln (8/7)\n\\]\n\nTherefore, \\(b = 8/7\\). The final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{8}{7}}\n\\]",
