      "text": "To solve the equation \\(7^{x+7} = 8^x\\), we start by taking the natural logarithm of both sides. This gives us:\n\n\\[\n\\ln(7^{x+7}) = \\ln(8^x)\n\\]\n\nUsing the logarithm power rule, \\(\\ln(a^b) = b \\ln(a)\\), we can rewrite the equation as:\n\n\\[\n(x+7) \\ln 7 = x \\ln 8\n\\]\n\nNext, we distribute \\(\\ln 7\\) on the left side:\n\n\\[\nx \\ln 7 + 7 \\ln 7 = x \\ln 8\n\\]\n\nTo isolate \\(x\\), we move all terms involving \\(x\\) to one side of the equation:\n\n\\[\nx \\ln 7 - x \\ln 8 = -7 \\ln 7\n\\]\n\nFactor out \\(x\\) on the left side:\n\n\\[\nx (\\ln 7 - \\ln 8) = -7 \\ln 7\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(x\\), we divide both sides by \\(\\ln 7 - \\ln 8\\):\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-7 \\ln 7}{\\ln 7 - \\ln 8}\n\\]\n\nWe are asked to express \\(x\\) in the form \\(x = \\log_b 7^7\\). Recall that \\(\\log_b a = \\frac{\\ln a}{\\ln b}\\). Therefore, we can write:\n\n\\[\nx = \\log_b 7^7 = \\frac{\\ln 7^7}{\\ln b}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\ln 7^7 = 7 \\ln 7\\), we have:\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{7 \\ln 7}{\\ln b}\n\\]\n\nBy comparing this with \\(x = \\frac{-7 \\ln 7}{\\ln 7 - \\ln 8}\\), we can see that they are equal. Thus, \\(\\ln b = \\ln 7 - \\ln 8\\). Therefore, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\ln b = \\ln \\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right)\n\\]\n\nExponentiating both sides to solve for \\(b\\), we get:\n\n\\[\nb = \\frac{7}{8}\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(b\\) is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{7}{8}}\\).",
