      "text": "To solve the equation \\(7^{x+7} = 8^x\\), we start by expressing both sides with the same base. However, since 7 and 8 are different bases, we can use logarithms to solve for \\(x\\).\n\nFirst, take the natural logarithm (or any logarithm, but we will use the natural logarithm for convenience) of both sides of the equation:\n\n\\[\n\\ln(7^{x+7}) = \\ln(8^x)\n\\]\n\nUsing the properties of logarithms, specifically \\(\\ln(a^b) = b \\ln(a)\\), we can rewrite the equation as:\n\n\\[\n(x+7) \\ln 7 = x \\ln 8\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to isolate \\(x\\). First, distribute \\(x\\) on the right side:\n\n\\[\nx \\ln 7 + 7 \\ln 7 = x \\ln 8\n\\]\n\nNow, move all terms involving \\(x\\) to one side of the equation:\n\n\\[\nx \\ln 7 - x \\ln 8 = -7 \\ln 7\n\\]\n\nFactor out \\(x\\) on the left side:\n\n\\[\nx (\\ln 7 - \\ln 8) = -7 \\ln 7\n\\]\n\nSolve for \\(x\\) by dividing both sides by \\(\\ln 7 - \\ln 8\\):\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-7 \\ln 7}{\\ln 7 - \\ln 8}\n\\]\n\nWe can simplify the denominator using the property of logarithms that \\(\\ln a - \\ln b = \\ln \\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)\\):\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-7 \\ln 7}{\\ln \\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right)}\n\\]\n\nGiven that \\(x = \\log_b 7^7\\), we can rewrite the equation as:\n\n\\[\n\\log_b 7^7 = \\frac{-7 \\ln 7}{\\ln \\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right)}\n\\]\n\nUsing the property of logarithms that \\(\\log_b a^c = c \\log_b a\\), we get:\n\n\\[\n\\log_b 7^7 = 7 \\log_b 7\n\\]\n\nSo, we have:\n\n\\[\n7 \\log_b 7 = \\frac{-7 \\ln 7}{\\ln \\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right)}\n\\]\n\nDivide both sides by 7:\n\n\\[\n\\log_b 7 = \\frac{-\\ln 7}{\\ln \\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right)}\n\\]\n\nTo find \\(b\\), we need to express \\(\\ln \\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right)\\) in terms of \\(\\ln 7\\). Using the logarithm property \\(\\ln \\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right) = \\ln a - \\ln b\\), we get:\n\n\\[\n\\ln \\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right) = \\ln 7 - \\ln 8\n\\]\n\nSo, the equation becomes:\n\n\\[\n\\log_b 7 = \\frac{-\\ln 7}{\\ln 7 - \\ln 8}\n\\]\n\nTh