      "text": "To solve the equation \\(7^{x+7} = 8^x\\), we start by expressing both sides with the same base. However, since 7 and 8 are not powers of the same base, we will take the logarithm of both sides to solve for \\(x\\).\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm (or any logarithm, but we'll use the natural logarithm for convenience):\n\\[\n\\ln(7^{x+7}) = \\ln(8^x)\n\\]\n\nUsing the logarithm power rule \\(\\ln(a^b) = b \\ln(a)\\), we get:\n\\[\n(x+7) \\ln 7 = x \\ln 8\n\\]\n\nNext, we distribute the logarithms:\n\\[\nx \\ln 7 + 7 \\ln 7 = x \\ln 8\n\\]\n\nTo isolate \\(x\\), we move all terms involving \\(x\\) to one side and the constants to the other side:\n\\[\nx \\ln 7 - x \\ln 8 = -7 \\ln 7\n\\]\n\nFactor out \\(x\\) on the left side:\n\\[\nx (\\ln 7 - \\ln 8) = -7 \\ln 7\n\\]\n\nNow, solve for \\(x\\) by dividing both sides by \\(\\ln 7 - \\ln 8\\):\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-7 \\ln 7}{\\ln 7 - \\ln 8}\n\\]\n\nWe can simplify the right side by recognizing that \\(\\ln 7 - \\ln 8 = \\ln \\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right)\\):\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-7 \\ln 7}{\\ln \\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right)}\n\\]\n\nRewriting \\(\\ln \\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right)\\) as \\(-\\ln \\left(\\frac{8}{7}\\right)\\), we get:\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-7 \\ln 7}{-\\ln \\left(\\frac{8}{7}\\right)} = \\frac{7 \\ln 7}{\\ln \\left(\\frac{8}{7}\\right)}\n\\]\n\nWe are given that the solution can be expressed in the form \\(x = \\log_b 7^7\\). Recall that \\(\\log_b a = \\frac{\\log_c a}{\\log_c b}\\) for any base \\(c\\). Here, we can write:\n\\[\nx = \\frac{\\log 7^7}{\\log b} = \\frac{7 \\log 7}{\\log b}\n\\]\n\nBy comparing \\(x = \\frac{7 \\ln 7}{\\ln \\left(\\frac{8}{7}\\right)}\\) with \\(x = \\frac{7 \\log 7}{\\log b}\\), we see that:\n\\[\n\\log b = \\ln \\left(\\frac{8}{7}\\right)\n\\]\n\nThus, \\(b = \\frac{8}{7}\\). Therefore, the value of \\(b\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{8}{7}}\n\\]",
